DP  - 2020 Aug
TI  - Natural Language Processing in Dutch Free Text Radiology Reports: Challenges in a 
      Small Language Area Staging Pulmonary Oncology.
PG  - 1002-1008
LID - 10.1007/s10278-020-00327-z [doi]
AB  - Reports are the standard way of communication between the radiologist and the 
      referring clinician. Efforts are made to improve this communication by, for 
      instance, introducing standardization and structured reporting. Natural Language 
      Processing (NLP) is another promising tool which can improve and enhance the 
      radiological report by processing free text. NLP as such adds structure to the 
      report and exposes the information, which in turn can be used for further 
      analysis. This paper describes pre-processing and processing steps and highlights 
      important challenges to overcome in order to successfully implement a free text 
      mining algorithm using NLP tools and machine learning in a small language area, 
      like Dutch. A rule-based algorithm was constructed to classify T-stage of 
      pulmonary oncology from the original free text radiological report, based on the 
      items tumor size, presence and involvement according to the 8th TNM 
      classification system. PyContextNLP, spaCy and regular expressions were used as 
      tools to extract the correct information and process the free text. Overall 
      accuracy of the algorithm for evaluating T-stage was 0,83 in the training set and 
      0,87 in the validation set, which shows that the approach in this pilot study is 
      promising. Future research with larger datasets and external validation is needed 
      to be able to introduce more machine learning approaches and perhaps to reduce 
      required input efforts of domain-specific knowledge. However, a hybrid NLP 
      approach will probably achieve the best results.
